# PLAN — Version-aware VM GPU feature restructure

Status: implementation approved for three candidate commits on
`feature/vm/gpu`. Remote node mutation remains a separate post-publication
human gate.

## 1. Current-state assessment

The previous feature concentrated argument parsing, platform detection, sysfs
inspection, boot-file rendering, `qm` mutation, rollback, and output formatting
in `setup/vm/gpu.sh`. That made each new action or Proxmox difference another
branch in a root-running script. Pages publication also knew specific runner
names, so a new dependency required changes in several case-oriented blocks.

The retained research plans correctly identified whole-slot discovery,
node-local BDFs, IOMMU isolation, exact rollback, stopped VMs, and PVE 6/PVE 9
differences. They also conflict in important places: some prescribe global
AMD+NVIDIA blacklisting, `amd_iommu=on`, always-early VFIO binding, automatic
desktop console removal, or support for PVE 7/8. Those instructions are not the
implemented contract.

The authoritative implementation now has these boundaries:

| Concern | Owner |
| --- | --- |
| download and dependency resolution | `setup/vm/gpu.sh` |
| parser, aliases, normalized state | `cli/gpu/common.sh` |
| authoritative platform and adapter detection | `cli/gpu/platform.sh` |
| complete PCI identity inventory and explicit selection | `cli/gpu/inventory.sh` |
| preflight, verify, status, inventory output | `cli/gpu/inspect.sh` |
| mutation request and release dispatch | `cli/gpu/apply.sh` |
| PVE 6.4/Buster capability contract | `ansible/release/6.4/gpu.yml` |
| PVE 9.x/Trixie capability contract | `ansible/release/9.1/gpu.yml` |
| host and VM transactions | `ansible/proxmox/helper/vm.gpu.yml` |
| publication inventory | `actions/pages.features.txt` |
| GPU fixture contract | `actions/validate.vm.gpu.sh` |

## 2. Goals

1. Keep the public bootstrap small and declarative.
2. Make read-only actions independently runnable without Ansible.
3. Detect the live platform and complete GPU inventory before passing a typed
   schema-v3 request to Ansible.
4. Keep release files thin: data describes capabilities; shared tasks implement
   behavior.
5. Revalidate release, PCI, IOMMU, VM, conflict, approval, and ownership facts
   at the mutation boundary.
6. Make host and VM changes reversible from root-only owned state.
7. Publish every runner dependency from declarations rather than workflow
   cases.
8. Preserve compatibility aliases while making canonical actions explicit.

## 3. Supported release lanes

### PVE 6.4 / Buster

- Detected from the complete live `pveversion`, Debian codename Buster, and
  boot/VFIO capabilities. The `6.4` lane name identifies the compatible
  adapter; it is not an assumed installed minor.
- Default and only supported binding strategy is `early`.
- `prepare` installs exact-BDF initramfs binding for every function in the
  physical slot.
- `vfio_virqfd` is included only when present on the installed kernel.
- GRUB, `pve-efiboot-tool`, and `proxmox-boot-tool` are runtime capabilities,
  not assumptions.
- The normal manual sequence is inventory, prepare dry-run, prepare, reboot,
  verify, attach, guest test, detach, unprepare, reboot.

### PVE 9.x / Trixie through the 9.1 lane

- Detected from the complete live `pveversion`, Debian codename Trixie, and
  boot/VFIO capabilities. Compatible future PVE 9 minors use the same adapter
  without a minor-version default.
- Default binding strategy is `automatic`.
- The normal manual sequence is inventory, preflight, attach dry-run, attach,
  guest test, and detach. No host files need to change when automatic handoff
  works.
- `--binding early` enables the same exact-address recovery strategy only after
  automatic handoff fails.
- `vfio_virqfd` is not requested.

### Deliberate refusals

PVE 7/Bullseye and PVE 8/Bookworm are documented by historic research but are
not automated in this patch. Unknown, missing, cross-matched, or explicitly
wrong release data fails closed.

`--release` is an assertion only. It can reject a detected lane mismatch but
can never choose a release wrapper. Unknown boot tooling also fails closed;
there is no implicit GRUB fallback.

No lane synthesizes `amd_iommu=on`. An Intel fallback is added only when live
IOMMU groups are absent. `iommu=pt` is independently opt-in.

## 4. Canonical action state machine

```text
inventory -> preflight -> [prepare -> reboot -> verify] -> attach
                                                        |
                                                        v
unprepare <- reboot <- host rollback <- detach <- guest validation
```

The bracketed host-preparation path is mandatory for PVE 6 early binding and
conditional for PVE 9.

### `inventory`

Read sysfs and return one record per display-controller slot plus every sibling
function. Record BDF, class, vendor/device IDs, subsystem IDs, current driver,
IOMMU group, NUMA node, boot-VGA state, reset support, and VM assignments.
Identical vendor/device IDs remain distinct because address is the identity.
An empty inventory succeeds; no GPU is selected automatically.

### `preflight`

Require a function-qualified supplied BDF, match it against complete live
inventory, discover every same-slot function, validate AMD or
NVIDIA identity, require real IOMMU groups, allow only selected functions and
PCI bridges in those groups, scan raw VM configs for conflicts, and validate an
optional target VM. A selected boot display requires explicit host-display-loss
acknowledgement. Blacklist mode additionally proves no unselected display GPU
uses the selected vendor family.

### `prepare`

Allow absent IOMMU groups only so firmware/kernel preparation can be previewed.
Refuse the AMD missing-IOMMU case because no valid AMD kernel token can replace
disabled firmware. Back up every managed path, apply only the selected binding,
blacklist, and kernel-token features, refresh the detected boot path and
initramfs, then persist schema-v3 ownership state with exact function identity
records. On failure, restore files,
refresh boot artifacts again, and return an explicit rollback outcome.

### `verify` / `--test`

Repeat preflight and apply state-aware driver expectations. Early binding
requires `vfio-pci` for every selected function. Automatic handoff permits the
native driver while the VM is stopped and expects VFIO ownership while it is
running.

### `attach` / `--attach`

Require a stopped eligible VM. Choose or validate `hostpci0..15`. Immediately
before mutation, the playbook independently revalidates the live release,
function existence, complete IOMMU isolation, early VFIO ownership, stopped VM,
free hostpci entry, and cross-VM references. Capture exact `vga` and `onboot`
values, add one whole-slot `hostpciN`, apply explicit primary/onboot features,
write ownership state, and optionally start the VM. Rescue removes partial
configuration and restores captured values.

An existing matching state/config pair returns `already-attached`; conflicting
owned state refuses mutation.

### `detach` / `--remove`

Require feature-owned state and a stopped VM. Revalidate that the recorded
`hostpciN` still contains the recorded slot, remove only that entry, restore
captured VM fields, then remove state. Rescue restores the owned hostpci value.
Physical PCI presence is not required for recovery.

### `status`

Report detected platform/adapter, complete inventory, owned host state, and an
optional explicit selection without mutation.

### `unprepare`

Require host state, no owned VM states, and no raw VM references. Validate GPU
ownership, bootloader stability, transaction path containment, and every
required backup before changing files. Restore original files or remove paths
that did not exist, support legacy schema-v1 cleanup, refresh boot/initramfs,
remove completed transaction data, and finally remove ownership state. The GPU
may be physically absent because state is the recovery source.

## 5. Dynamic feature flags

The runner serializes requested behavior as data under
`gpu_request.requested_features`; release wrappers decide which values are
allowed; shared tasks consume them. Adding a bounded flag therefore requires:

1. parse and validate it in `cli/gpu/common.sh`;
2. include a typed boolean/string in `cli/gpu/apply.sh`;
3. declare capability constraints in the release wrapper when version-specific;
4. implement one guarded task path in the shared playbook;
5. add request, negative, idempotence, and rollback fixture assertions;
6. document its safety acknowledgement and recovery effect.

Current data flags are binding strategy, vendor blacklist, primary GPU,
disable-onboot, IOMMU passthrough-domain mode, start VM, and reboot host.
Approvals are separate data fields so the playbook can independently reject an
unsafe request rather than trusting a shell-only check.

## 6. State and transaction contract

The default state root is
`/etc/ansible/proxmox/gpu-passthrough`, mode `0700`. State files and generated
results use mode `0600`.

Host state owns the release lane, selected display BDF, slot, complete function
identity records, binding,
blacklist vendor, IOMMU mode, bootloader, transaction directory, and an
original-file manifest. Each original record stores path, prior existence,
checksum, and backup. Idempotent prepare requires the same material feature
contract; a changed contract requires unprepare and a reviewed new prepare.

VM state owns the VM ID, selected display BDF, complete function identity
records, GPU slot, hostpci index/value, original `vga`, original `onboot`, and
which fields were managed. State is removed only after the corresponding
transaction completes. Absent-hardware recovery refuses incomplete legacy
state rather than inventing a `.0` function.

A nonblocking feature lock serializes all mutations. Dry-run uses a temporary
lock and does not create the persistent state root.

## 7. Publication restructure

`actions/pages.features.txt` is the single source-to-destination manifest for
setup runners. Policy `feature` means the builder reads
`FEATURE_PLAYBOOKS`, optional `FEATURE_SUPPORT_FILES`, and optional
`FEATURE_CLI_FILES` from the runner. Policy `plain` publishes only the runner.

The Pages builder copies declared dependencies and assigns executable mode only
to standalone CLI scripts. The validator compares every published declaration
and follows `import_playbook` edges, including both GPU release wrappers to the
shared playbook. Workflow YAML no longer contains a GPU-branch publication
case.

## 8. Validation plan

The focused fixture must cover:

- Bash syntax and declared dependency existence;
- generic publication graph markers;
- canonical actions and compatibility aliases;
- authoritative platform helper output and assertion-only release flags;
- compatible future PVE 9 minor dispatch and unknown-platform refusal;
- empty inventory and function-qualified BDF enforcement;
- complete all-GPU inventory serialization into Ansible extra-vars;
- identical AMD GPUs kept distinct by BDF;
- complete selected slot across independent safe IOMMU groups;
- PVE 9 automatic and PVE 6 early binding dispatch;
- explicit release mismatch refusal;
- same-vendor blacklist collateral refusal;
- typed feature request serialization and IOMMU facts;
- macOS plus NVIDIA refusal;
- mixed-group, running/HA/locked VM, occupied hostpci, boot-VGA, absent hardware
  recovery, stale state, and invalid bootloader negatives;
- host/attach/detach/unprepare transaction markers;
- Ansible syntax on Linux CI.

Repository acceptance also requires `git diff --check`, all shell syntax,
release validators, a Linux Pages build, local Pages artifact validation, and
the repository runtime validator. This macOS workstation has Bash 3.2, while
the pre-existing Pages scripts require Bash 4 associative arrays; the Pages
execution gate therefore remains a Linux/CI requirement.

## 9. Human-review and remote-test boundary

This patch stops before commit, push, Pages dispatch, reboot, or remote VM
mutation. A reviewer should inspect:

- every managed host path and restore rule;
- both release contracts and release refusal behavior;
- exact-BDF initramfs rendering;
- vendor-specific blacklist scope and collateral refusal;
- VM field capture, idempotence, and rescue order;
- manifest-driven Pages output and dependency graph;
- JSON schemas and exit codes;
- the updated operator runbook.

After approval, create three logical commits for runtime, CI/publication, and
documentation, then push only the exact final feature tip. Follow
`prompt/core/publish.feature.script.plan`: validate the
candidate read-only, dispatch the trusted workflow from `main` with exact
`source_ref`, full `source_sha`, and typed confirmation, then verify the Pages
checksum. Download and inspect the runner before testing mutations.

Remote acceptance is two separate rollback-capable lanes:

1. PVE 9/Trixie: inventory, preflight, automatic attach dry-run/apply, guest
   detection, stop/start reset test, detach, and host-driver recovery.
2. PVE 6.4/Buster: inventory, prepare dry-run/apply, reboot, verify, attach,
   guest detection, stop/start reset test, detach, unprepare, reboot, and exact
   host-file/driver restoration.

Restore `main` through the same trusted Pages workflow immediately after the
test window or at any stop condition. Never force-push `www`, mix source SHAs,
or test a bootstrap against dependencies from another snapshot.

## 10. Out of scope for this patch

No support is claimed for PVE 7/8, LXC GPU exposure, iGPU splitting, vGPU,
resource mappings, live migration, ACS override, unsafe interrupts, ROM files,
guest driver installation, interactive menus, automated firmware changes, or
automatic GPU-reset remediation. Each requires a separate capability contract,
fixtures, operator recovery path, and review.
